<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oder–Neisse line</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oder–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a> line is the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, following the Oder and Lusatian Neisse rivers. Agreed provisionally at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a> Conference in 1945, it placed the former German lands to the east under Polish and Soviet administration, and millions of Germans were expelled from them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> recognised it in 1950 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> in 1970, and reunited Germany confirmed it by treaty in 1990.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oder-neisse-line.jpg" alt="Oder–Neisse line" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oder-neisse.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
